!!! Cryptographically secure random values.
!!!
!!! nil.std.math is MINSTD, seeded at 12345, and says itself that it is not
!!! cryptographic. Before nil.std.security's random intrinsics there was no
!!! alternative anywhere in the standard library, and /dev/urandom was no way
!!! round it: the file intrinsics refuse a character device by design. Any
!!! library needing a session id, a token or a nonce had to either invent
!!! something predictable or drop the feature.
!!!
!!! Nothing here can assert a particular value, which is the point. What it
!!! asserts is the shape, the bounds, and that two draws differ. The last is
!!! only a constant-output sanity check; it does not prove cryptographic quality.

book matrix.entropy

read nil.std.core
read nil.std.text.string_utils
read nil.std.security.random

!!! Reports a Result<text> as a width, or minus one if it failed, so the
!!! expected output does not depend on the bytes drawn.
hex-width receives byte-count as number returns number begin paragraph
    value drawn is nil.std.security::random_hex with byte_count being byte-count
    consider drawn
        when Result.Ok holding token then give nil.std.text::length with s being token
        otherwise give 0 minus 1
    end consider
end paragraph

hex-of receives byte-count as number returns text begin paragraph
    value drawn is nil.std.security::random_hex with byte_count being byte-count
    consider drawn
        when Result.Ok holding token then give token
        otherwise give ""
    end consider
end paragraph

!!! Whether every character is a lower-case hex digit. A generator that
!!! returned raw bytes through the hex door would fail here.
all-hex receives token as text returns boolean begin paragraph
    value width is nil.std.text::length with s being token
    when width is less than 1 then
        give false
    end when
    variable index as number is 0
    repeat while index is less than width
        value code is nil.std.text::byte_at with s being token, index being index
        value is-digit is code is at least 48 and code is at most 57
        value is-lower is code is at least 97 and code is at most 102
        when not (is-digit or is-lower) then
            give false
        end when
        set index to index plus 1
    end repeat
    give true
end paragraph

main returns number begin paragraph
    !!! Two hex characters per byte, which is what makes the width predictable
    !!! even though the content is not.
    say hex-width with byte-count being 16
    say hex-width with byte-count being 32

    !!! The bounds are refused rather than clamped: a caller asking for zero
    !!! bytes of entropy has a bug, and so has one asking for a megabyte.
    say hex-width with byte-count being 0
    say hex-width with byte-count being 1025

    value token is hex-of with byte-count being 16
    say (all-hex with token being token) becomes text

    !!! The check that matters. Two draws from a secure source differ; two
    !!! draws from a counter or a seeded LCG at a fixed start do not.
    say (not ((hex-of with byte-count being 16) equals (hex-of with byte-count being 16))) becomes text

    !!! Raw bytes come back as text and are read by byte, not by character.
    value raw is nil.std.security::random_bytes with byte_count being 8
    consider raw
        when Result.Ok holding bytes then say nil.std.text::length with s being bytes
        otherwise say 0 minus 1
    end consider

    !!! A bounded draw lands inside its bound, every time.
    variable inside as boolean is true
    variable round as number is 0
    repeat while round is less than 50
        value picked is nil.std.security::random_below with bound being 10
        consider picked
            when Result.Ok holding amount then begin block
                when amount is less than 0 or amount is at least 10 then
                    set inside to false
                end when
            end block
            otherwise set inside to false
        end consider
        set round to round plus 1
    end repeat
    say inside becomes text

    !!! A bound of one has exactly one answer, and a bound below one is a bug.
    value only is nil.std.security::random_below with bound being 1
    consider only
        when Result.Ok holding amount then say amount
        otherwise say 0 minus 1
    end consider
    value impossible is nil.std.security::random_below with bound being 0
    consider impossible
        when Result.Ok holding amount then say amount
        otherwise say "refused"
    end consider
    give 0
end paragraph
